  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Virginia has $85.5B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 29.1% year over year. Award activity in Virginia includes 300,276 awards with an average award size of $285K. The largest single buyer in the state is the Department of Veterans Affairs, which obligated $31.6B, followed by the Department of Defense, which obligated $29.6B. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest industry by obligations, with $28.6B, and Computer Systems Design Services follows with $8.4B. OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. is the largest vendor in the state with $19.9B in obligations, and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP ranks second with $8.7B. Historical context shows obligations by fiscal year peaked at $121B in FY25 and then moved to $85.5B in FY26 to date. All totals are contract obligations and FY2026 totals are partial, fiscal year to date. This snapshot highlights concentration in health-related buying and large prime vendors serving federal health programs in Virginia.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 29.1% year over year. Virginia recorded $85.5B in obligations in FY2026 to date after a peak of $121B in FY25. Award activity continued at scale with 300,276 awards and an average award size of $285K. The shift largely reflects lower current-year obligations compared with the prior fiscal year totals."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate Virginia contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $31.6B and the Department of Defense obligated $29.6B, making them the two largest buyers in the state. OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. received $19.9B and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP received $8.7B, placing both vendors among the top recipients. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers received $28.6B in obligations, underscoring health-related contracting concentration."
      }
    ]
  },
